!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/reader-sidecar.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/35/m/core.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/sidecar-common.tn"
@doc:"""
  Formal shape of a reader-layer sidecar (tests/class1/reader/**/*-expected.tn) -- see README.md's
  own "The reader layer" section and RUNNER.md.

  The layer exists because §1.2 puts a set of rules below neither tier: the token stream and the
  structural parser both decline to dedupe fields or keys, resolve an empty brace, or interpret
  token text, so a lexer- or parser-bucket vector structurally cannot fail on §2.5's unique field
  names, §2.6's key identity, §2.8's empty brace or §2.9's absent-key restriction. This is the layer
  where a Class 1 document gets its verdict, and the first that can.

  A `reader_value` is what a schemaless read produces, so it differs from the parser layer's
  core_value in three ways that are the point rather than an accident: an empty brace is gone
  (§2.8 resolves it), a duplicate is gone (§2.5/§2.6 reject it), and a token has become a resolved
  base value (§4). It carries no annotations -- annotation capture is the parser layer's to state,
  and nothing here needs them.
  """
{
  @doc:"One field of a record, after §2.5's uniqueness rule has been applied."
  reader_field => {
    name: text
    value: reader_value
  }

  @doc:"One entry of a map, after §2.6's key-identity rule has been applied."
  reader_entry => {
    key: reader_value
    value: reader_value
  }

  reader_record => { fields: [reader_field] }
  reader_map    => { entries: [reader_entry] }
  reader_array  => { elements: [reader_value] }

  @doc:"""
    A leaf, named by the base type §4 resolved it to. A number is written as a decimal string and
    compared by numeric value, not by spelling -- §4.3 leaves the host type an implementation
    concern, so `0xFF` and `255` are one value here and a vector cannot ask which was written.
    That is the resolver layer's question, not this one.

    There are three members because §4 resolves three classes. The token `null` is a string like
    any other unquoted token that matched neither the boolean nor the number rule, and absence is
    not a base type at all: `_` is lexical, and reaches a tree through `absent` below.
    """
  reader_atom => {
    ( boolean: boolean
    | string: text
    | number: text )
  }

  @doc:"""
    One value of the tree a schemaless read produces. `absent` is the sentinel occupying a position
    (§2.9): it is a value, distinct from the position not being there, which is why it is a member
    here rather than an omission.
    """
  reader_value => {
    ( record: reader_record
    | map: reader_map
    | array: reader_array
    | atom: reader_atom
    | absent: void )
  }

  reader_valid => {
    value: reader_value
  }

  @doc:"""
    An `error` vector's category is `resolver` throughout this layer: §2.9 names it outright for an
    absent map key, and §8.1's resolver bullet already houses the data-format rules a processor
    applies once the structural parser has accepted the document. §2.5 and §2.6 require rejection of
    a duplicate without assigning a category at all, and §8.1's own lists do not mention duplicates
    -- an open question this corpus answers the same way for both.
    """
  reader_sidecar => sidecar_common & {
    ( valid: reader_valid | error: vector_error | refused: vector_refusal )
  }
}
